# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for run.sh's tsan mode. A report whose every access is inside Mesa
# (lavapipe) or SDL's own threads gets a line here, with the run that showed it and why it is not
# StreamFlex's; a report with a frame in src/ in an access stack is never suppressed. A frame of ours in
# the stack that created a thread or a mutex does not count: SDL creates its renderer from
# create_window, so every lavapipe thread and mutex is made under it.

# Task 1.13's tsan run at 6694f8a (R12), the unmutated control: 9 runs (f30-mixed, f30-vanish, f60-icons,
# f60-nothreads, f60-selected, f60-xpm, f60-xpm-spin, h-order, h-slow), each with two data races on one
# llvmpipe fence, named with mesa-vulkan-drivers-dbgsym (Mesa 25.0.7-2+deb13u1). lavapipe's first
# thread (the queue submit thread lvp_queue_init starts first) destroys the fence's mutex in lp_fence_destroy (lp_fence.c:130)
# against a rasterizer thread's (llvmpipe-N) lock of it in mtx_lock, and the fence's condition variable
# (lp_fence.c:131) against another rasterizer thread's broadcast on it in cnd_broadcast; the fence was
# allocated in lp_fence_create. The two addresses are the mutex and the condition variable of struct
# lp_fence, which only lp_fence_signal broadcasts. No frame of src/ is in either access stack.
# Mesa's cause: each rasterizer thread locks the fence, counts and broadcasts in lp_fence_signal, and
# the thread that drops the last reference destroys it. llvmpipe orders the two through an unlocked
# read of the count and an atomic reference count, and lavapipe is not built with ThreadSanitizer, so it
# sees neither. The unlocked read is in lp_fence_signalled (Mesa's own name; spelling: allow). Upstream
# ignores that function and lp_fence_signal in Mesa's own ThreadSanitizer builds for the unlocked read
# (tsan-blacklist.txt, commit 7c36c4f0a418, merge request 28650). No upstream fix for lp_fence_destroy
# was found; Mesa's issue tracker was not searched.
race:^lp_fence_destroy$
